agent	Parse MCP Gateway logs for step summary	2026-10-22T02:34:01.7929390Z {"timestamp":"2026-10-22T02:33:54.676Z","event":"rpc_request","_schema":"rpc-message/v2","direction":"OUT","server_id":"safeoutputs","method":"tools/call","payload":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"tools/call","params":{"arguments":{"body":"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\n\n**Reproduction:** Black 25.11.0 (default flags): reformatted as you describe, no error. Black 26.1.0 (latest at issue date): same output. Black's built-in safety check (AST equivalence) passed in both runs.\n**Assessment:** The reformatting only splits the line. It doesn't change meaning. In Python, a conditional expression has lower precedence than `==`, so `my_var == 999 if abc == \"TEST\" else 0` already parses as `(my_var == 999) if abc == \"TEST\" else 0` in both the original and the formatted code. When `abc == \"TEST\"` the assertion tests `my_var == 999`, not `my_var == 0` as the report says. The suggested form with `my_var == (999 if ... else 0)` is a different expression. Black does not add parentheses that change grouping.\n**Next step:** If you see a case where the formatted code behaves differently, please share the exact input, the output, and how you observed the difference. Otherwise this looks like expected behaviour.\n\n\u003csub\u003eblack-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: `R: not a bug`\u003c/sub\u003e","item_number":"11"},"name":"add_comment"}}}
